From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] windows: pack layers before the guest runs out of interrupt lines

The platform-independent series raises gptLayerThreshold from 8 to 20, to keep
ordinary images off the packed path while that path did not work. On x86-64
that number is above what the guest's interrupt controller can carry.

An IOAPIC has 24 pins and the first five belong to hardware the guest expects
to find, so virtio gets nineteen. A sandbox spends six or seven of those before
its first image layer. A 17-layer image therefore asks for 24 devices, and
libkrun refuses to build the VM at all:

    Building the microVM failed: RegisterBlockDevice(IrqsExhausted)

which reaches the caller as `krun_start_enter failed: -22`. Measured on Windows
on 2026-08-25, on an image that runs on an Apple Silicon Mac, whose interrupt
controller has no such ceiling. This is the exact failure the note at the end
of packaging/libkrun-windows/patches/0036 predicted.

Ten rather than twelve: the non-layer device count was six in one boot and
seven in another, the difference being one more share, so the budget is not a
constant to compute against exactly. Ten leaves the flat path to images that
fit comfortably and packs the rest.

This is only viable together with packaging/imago/. Until that fix, the packed
path answered every partition past the third with zeros reported as a
successful read, so lowering the threshold alone would trade one failure for
another.

Applies on top of packaging/nerdbox/patches/0002, which is where the 20 comes
from; the Windows series is applied second, as packaging/nerdbox/README.md
describes.
---
diff --git a/internal/shim/task/mount.go b/internal/shim/task/mount.go
index c2ca2fa..8957843 100644
--- a/internal/shim/task/mount.go
+++ b/internal/shim/task/mount.go
@@ -45,7 +45,26 @@ import (
 // devices total, some of which are reserved by the VM implementation) and
 // lets the shim handle deep stacks of independent erofs mounts without
 // coordinating layer offsets in the snapshotter.
-const gptLayerThreshold = 20
+// Windows runs on x86-64, where the guest's interrupt controller is an IOAPIC with 24 pins.
+// Pins 0-4 belong to hardware the guest expects to find -- the PIT, the i8042, the 8259
+// cascade and the 16550s -- so virtio gets 5 through 23: nineteen devices, and that is a
+// property of the interrupt controller rather than a tunable
+// (packaging/libkrun-windows/patches/0036).
+//
+// A sandbox spends six or seven of those before its first image layer: console, RNG, balloon,
+// NIC, virtiofs, the runtime's config disk and its writable ext4 layer. Measured at six in one
+// boot and seven in another, the difference being an extra share. Ten leaves the flat path --
+// the one with all the mileage -- to images that fit comfortably, and packs the rest, with
+// slack for a sandbox that mounts another volume.
+//
+// Above this the shim packs, and past 19 devices the VM does not start at all: libkrun reports
+// RegisterBlockDevice(IrqsExhausted), which surfaces through the C API as krun_start_enter
+// failed: -22. Measured on Windows on 2026-08-25 with a 17-layer image at threshold 20.
+//
+// Packing is only viable because of packaging/imago/: libkrun's VMDK reader answered every
+// partition past the third with zeros-reported-as-success until that fix. A Windows shim
+// carrying this threshold and a krun.dll without that fix is the worst of both worlds.
+const gptLayerThreshold = 10
 
 // diskAllocator assigns sequential virtio disk letters starting after any
 // disks reserved by the VM implementation (see vm.Manager.ReservedDisks).
